From d1e5433763835b0814c2d9d4a636b84a82dd54ee Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gemini Mainline Experiment <gemini-mainline@example.invalid>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 21:34:38 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 167/167] wifi: mt6797: transmit mac80211's protected data
 frames on a finite TC1 page pool

During the data phase, admit mac80211's ordinary data to the target: a
non-QoS To-DS data frame with the Protected bit (the keys are in the
firmware, so mac80211 leaves no IV space and sets the bit itself), an
LLC/SNAP header, not EAPOL (no rekey on this path), queued at most eight
frames or 12400 bytes deep. The worker converts each to Ethernet II exactly
as it converts the control-port EAPOL frames (destination from address 3,
source from address 2, type from the SNAP header) and submits it on TC1
with the pinned nicTxComposeDesc shape for a protected data frame: LMAC
port 0 and queue index 1 (the arTcResourceControl row for TC1), long
format, non-802.11 header with the Ethernet II flag, TID 0 (no WMM was
negotiated), the protected-frame bit so the firmware encrypts with the
installed pairwise key under the station's WLAN index, own-MAC 1, the
bounded lifetime and attempt count, the fixed OFDM 6 Mbps rate and a PID
with TX status to the MCU. The reference run 3 ledger shows the vendor's
data in this format with the protected flag. One frame is in flight at a
time, across management, security and data.

The host-interface ledger gains the TC1 pool: a declared 16-page budget
(one 1518-byte frame with its descriptor is 13 pages, far below the pinned
NIC_TX_PAGE_COUNT_TC1), admitted once after TC4, debited at submission and
reconciled from the AC1 released count in WTQCR word 0's high half (HIF TX
index 1) matched against the shared free-for-all count, as the pinned
nicTxReleaseResource accrues each queue's count to its TC and turns them
into free pages from the pool: TC4 first, then TC1, a chosen deterministic
order when the pool is scarce; remainders stay pending; every other queue's
count, and any count above its debt, aborts the ledger as before. The idle
test now also requires the pool whole. The join guard retires the data
pages from the TC1 release and refuses an overflow.

A data frame the AP does not acknowledge is reported to mac80211 and
counted, never a fault by itself; eight unacknowledged in a row end the
data phase (reason txfail). The first submitted data frame is recorded by
PID, pages and length; the data phase end record carries the queued,
acknowledged and failed counts next to the receive counts. Frames still
queued when the phase ends are discarded unsent.
Two bounds complete the pool and the PID budget. The pending free-for-all
count serves either TC, so the ledger bounds it by the TC4 and TC1 debts
together (a pool count may precede or follow the queue count it frees);
an AC1 count without a TC1 debt still aborts. PIDs 1 to 127 are consumed in
order by management, security and data frames; ordinary data stops at 126,
ending the phase with reason pidbudget, so the reserved deauthentication
always has PID 127 and the teardown stays possible.

Unsigned experiment draft; archive identity is non-certifying.
